{"kind":"request","text":"Design note for review: record and event identifiers (notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md at 88056d593775371a76cb902df7da9ffadc1cddc3, draft 4, branch planner/record-identifiers-20261019). The first protocol factored out of dap for independent implementations. Part one stands alone: a record is identified by the digest of its genesis as did:seq:\u003cformat\u003e:\u003cgenesis\u003e, an event is cited as did:seq:\u003cformat\u003e:\u003cgenesis\u003e/\u003cposition\u003e/\u003cevent\u003e, an address is a URL or a Git remote kept apart from the identity; a format is defined by nine parts and dap1 and git are defined in full, at and nostr registered with definitions owed; four verification levels, with membership at level 2 and the signature rule from the genesis, and judgment outside; the three reads with HTTP and Git bindings and a record document; forks, discovery and privacy as security considerations; vectors from independent code. Part two: dap adopts in one change with no compatibility layer, references become { scope, rec } and { at, seq, hash }, incarnation and kind leave references, scope ID stays as the address; cost for gitseq, atseq, noseq and the Open Integrity Forge; the signed request as the next protocol. Decided by Hugh on 19 October 2026: the method name seq, and the scope ID kept in the reference. Reviewed before filing by an outsider's read of draft 2 and a readability pass. Design only: no source change in this head, nothing commissioned.","body":{"conditions":"Done when the checker approves an exact head of notes/2026-10-19-record-and-event-identifiers.md and the head lands on main. Design only: no runtime change in this head. The checker reads part one as a standalone protocol an outsider could implement, and part two as dap's plan; names anything an implementer would still have to guess, anything unnecessary, and any level that reaches less than its table says.","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b9